<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Quality control and expected frequencies prediction in manufacturing</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A light bulb factory in Eldoret tests its products for quality control. Historical data shows that <b>{@defective_rate@}%</b> of the bulbs produced in a standard run are defective.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Find the probability that a randomly selected light bulb from this factory is <b>working</b> (not defective).</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as an exact decimal).</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{Working}) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> If the factory produces a daily batch of <b>{@batch_size@}</b> bulbs, how many bulbs are expected to be <b>defective</b>?</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a whole number).</i></p>
<p>\(\text{Expected Defective} =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> In that same daily batch of <b>{@batch_size@}</b> bulbs, how many bulbs are expected to be <b>working</b> and ready for sale?</p>
<p>\(\text{Expected Working} =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>First, identify the baseline probability of a defect:<br>
\(\displaystyle P(\text{Defective}) = \frac{{@defective_rate@}}{100} = {@P_D@}\)</p>

<p><b>Part a: Probability of a Working Bulb</b><br>
A bulb is either defective or working. We use the complement rule to find the probability that a bulb works:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle P(\text{Working}) = 1 - P(\text{Defective})\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Working}) = 1 - {@P_D@} = {@ta1@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{Working}) = {@ta1@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Expected Defective Bulbs</b><br>
The expected frequency is the probability of the event multiplied by the total number of trials (the batch size).</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \text{Expected Defective} = P(\text{Defective}) \times \text{Total Bulbs}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle \text{Expected Defective} = {@P_D@} \times {@batch_size@} = {@ta2@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Expected Defective} = {@ta2@} \text{ bulbs} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: Expected Working Bulbs</b><br>
You can find this in two ways. Either multiply the probability of a working bulb by the total batch size, or simply subtract the expected defective bulbs from the total batch.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;"><b>Method 1:</b> \( {@ta1@} \times {@batch_size@} = {@ta3@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;"><b>Method 2:</b> \( {@batch_size@} - {@ta2@} = {@ta3@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Expected Working} = {@ta3@} \text{ bulbs} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
